docs/dump-hierarchy.md:57:`grantiva runner dump-hierarchy` and the MCP server's hierarchy tool share the same discovery as `grantiva hierarchy`: when no `grantiva runner start` session is active in the project, both fall back to the newest live `--keep-alive` session (`dump-hierarchy --udid <UDID or adb serial>` selects a specific simulator or emulator). With neither, all three fail with the same message (exit 1): ``Error: No active runner session. Start one with `grantiva runner start` or `grantiva run --keep-alive`, or pass --port.`` from `runner dump-hierarchy`; `grantiva hierarchy`, which has no `--port`, omits ", or pass --port"; the MCP tools also name the project's `.grantiva/session.json` and directory. While a session is still starting, they say so and to retry instead; [mcp.md](mcp.md#a-session-that-is-starting) gives the texts and which command prints which. An unknown `--format` is a usage error (exit 64) before any session is looked up.
README.md:98:- **Concurrent runs** — Runs on different simulator UDIDs execute in parallel. Each simulator launches WebDriverAgent from its own runner home (`<runner home>/versions/<stamp>/devices/<udid>`, where the runner home is `GRANTIVA_RUNNER_HOME` or `~/.grantiva/runner` and `<stamp>` is the installed runner's stamp: the runner version plus the bundled Android driver tag, such as `1.1.28-grantiva.13+android-drivers-2`), which holds an APFS clone of the cached WDA build, so every run gets its own xctestrun, port, and DerivedData without rebuilding WDA. `simulator teardown` (with or without `--force`) and `simulator delete` remove the device's home under every installed `versions/<stamp>/`, and teardown exits non-zero naming any home it could not remove; homes of simulators that no longer exist are pruned on the next run. A second run targeting an already-owned simulator fails immediately with guidance to provision a unique simulator, protecting the active WDA session from cross-run teardown; when the owner is a `grantiva runner start` session the message says so and names `grantiva runner stop`. A run's lease file (`<runner home>/locks/<udid>.lock`) is removed when the run ends, and a deleted simulator's when it is deleted.
README.md:196:| `GRANTIVA_RUNNER_HOME` | Runner directory, default `~/.grantiva/runner`. It holds simulator `locks/`, the WebDriverAgent build `cache/`, and one runner install per version under `versions/<stamp>/`; a home other than the default also holds its own `simulator-capacity/` (see Simulators Grantiva created). When an install is needed (no install of this version yet), grantiva first checks the `grantiva_GrantivaCore.bundle` beside the binary: a bundle from another release fails the install, names the bundle and either both runner versions or the tarball it lacks, leaves any existing install untouched, and leaves no empty `versions/<stamp>/` directory. An existing install of the current version is used as it is, without reading the bundle; `grantiva runner install` then warns when the bundle does not carry that runner, and every command that uses the runner warns (once) when no bundle is beside the binary at all, since the next fresh install would fail. A relative path is resolved against the current directory, and the home is always printed in its resolved form (`/private/tmp/…`, not `/tmp/…`), whether or not it exists yet. |
